## What the export shows

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix.
Its rendered cause table disagrees. The table below reads the report export and
tenant directory at render time, deduplicates report IDs, and classifies each
report by tenant locale, channel, and form version. Intake tags only describe
what intake guessed from the reporter's browser.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The **parser** reads day-first tenant dates month first. The **hint** on booking-v3
asks people to type month first, but the form interprets the entry day first.
These require separate tickets and independently verified fixes. The repository's
issue drafts and report-ID membership files record the export snapshot; rerun the
analysis and rebuild this page when data changes.

<Callout>
The five reports footnoted in the table remain in their classifier-assigned group.
Their bodies mention one-day shifts, timezone differences, or an invite arriving
before booking. Those symptoms require investigation; the export does not prove
a third cause or prove that a parser fix resolves them.
</Callout>

## When reports arrived, and when the desk merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The two series measure different events. The large merge batch is not a burst of
new failures. Reports arrived before that batch and continued afterward, including
near the export cutoff. “Known” is a workflow label, not evidence that incoming
reports stopped or that either failure was fixed. This export is a dated snapshot;
check newer queue data before relying on these counts today.

## Proposed replacement for decision 0001

This record proposes superseding [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
and its [subject-based triage instructions](/kb/triage/dedupe). The accepted record
is preserved as history. Its old review date is another reason to check the current
behavior rather than treating the prose as current evidence.

Read the report body and resolve the tenant before merging. Compare the tenant's
locale, channel, deployed form, symptoms, and known causal behavior. Merge reports
with the same established cause even if subjects differ; keep different causes
separate even if subjects match. Missing tenant information means investigate,
not automatically merge. Preserve a report's ID and count it once.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer runs the same classification logic as the cause table. It demonstrates
why the old subject rule can both split a single cause and merge different causes.
Classification supports triage; conflicting symptoms still deserve investigation.

## Follow-through

Keep one issue for the shared parser and one for the booking-v3 hint. Each issue
records its report count, evidence, reproduction, and checks. Link future reports
to the matching cause, retain uncertain cases for investigation, and review this
page after the next export or a verified fix. Do not describe either issue as
resolved until its own acceptance checks pass.

